{"step_index":113,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:33:34Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:33:34+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:33:34+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/prover_eval/w16.py`\nTotal Lines: 354\nTotal Bytes: 15815\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/usr/bin/env python\n2: \"\"\"tools/prover_eval/w16.py — whole-proof Pass@16, one model, no feedback.\n3: \n4: W16 is the condition that compares against the conventional whole-proof\n5: literature: 16 independent attempts, no compiler feedback, no retrieval, no\n6: cross-attempt communication, and a challenge the model may not edit.\n7: \n8: Each benchmark item supplies *two* declarations over one shared `Candidate`,\n9: so a model may attempt either direction and the item counts as resolved when\n10: either is kernel-verified. Which direction verified is recorded, not collapsed:\n11: against a labelled gold item, a verified proof of the wrong direction is not a\n12: success but evidence that the formalization is unsound, and that is the single\n13: most valuable signal this run can produce.\n14: \n15: Two guards matter more than the score:\n16: \n17:   * every accepted proof is re-checked with the axiom probe, so a `sorryAx`\n18:     reached through a helper lemma cannot pass as a proof;\n19:   * identical candidate proofs are verified once, because 16 samples of a\n20:     deterministic-ish model repeat heavily and Lean is the expensive part.\n21: \n22: Usage:\n23:     python tools/prover_eval/w16.py --model oprover-32b --partition gold\n24: \"\"\"\n25: from __future__ import annotations\n26: \n27: import argparse\n28: import concurrent.futures as cf\n29: import json\n30: import os\n31: import re\n32: import sys\n33: import threading\n34: import time\n35: \n36: sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(\n37:     os.path.abspath(__file__)))))\n38: \n39: from con\n<truncated 843 bytes>\n captured body began \"4\\n...\" and failed\n72: # every downstream check. OProver-8B writes ```lean4 in 60% of replies and\n73: # DeepSeek-7B in 57%, while Goedel-32B writes plain ```lean in 96%, so the bug\n74: # penalised models by which tag they happened to use. Re-scoring every saved\n75: # reply through the fix moved candidate counts a lot (DeepSeek 72 -> 102,\n76: # OProver-8B 11 -> 25) but changed no model's resolution count: Goedel-32B still\n77: # 3, everyone else still 0. The ranking was not an artefact of this regex.\n78: _FENCE = re.compile(r\"```(?:lean4|lean)?[^\\S\\n]*\\n?(.*?)```\", re.S)\n79: # Reasoning models (OProver and Goedel are Qwen3-style) spend tokens inside\n80: # <think> before answering. Left in place it swamps the parse — and if the token\n81: # budget expires mid-thought there is no answer at all, which is why the budget\n82: # has to be generous for these models rather than the 4096 a one-shot prover\n83: # would need.\n84: _THINK = re.compile(r\"<think>.*?</think>\", re.S)\n85: _OPEN_THINK = re.compile(r\"<think>.*\", re.S)\n86: _BY_TAIL = re.compile(r\"^\\s*(by\\b.*)$\", re.S)\n87: # Anything that would make a \"proof\" vacuous. The axiom probe catches these\n88: # even when they are reached indirectly, but rejecting them at the source is\n89: # cheaper and gives a clearer status than a failed audit.\n90: _BANNED = re.compile(r\"\\b(sorry|admit|axiom|unsafe|native_decide)\\b\")\n91: \n92: \n93: def extract_proof(text: str):\n94:     \"\"\"The proof term from a model reply, or None.\n95: \n96:     Tried in order of how much the reply respected the contract: an explicit\n97:     <answer> block, then a fenced Lean block, then a bare `by ...` tail. The\n98:     fallbacks matter — scoring a model as zero